# Cursor + Codex Ownership

One implementation owner per task per working tree.

Cursor and Codex must not independently modify the same implementation area in the same worktree at the same time.

## Cursor-owned

Cursor may inspect, modify, test, and commit.

Codex may review, analyze, investigate, or implement only from a different worktree/branch.

## Codex-owned

Treat Codex's active files as protected. Do not format, restructure, revert, or rewrite them in this worktree.

Cursor may still inspect, review, search, explain, and identify issues.

Parallel Cursor implementation requires an isolated branch/worktree.

## Transfer ownership

Before switching implementation owner:

1. `git status` and `git diff`
2. Run relevant validation
3. Checkpoint when the slice is coherent
4. Write a handoff (OWNER, TASK, BRANCH, WORKTREE, COMMIT, FILES CHANGED, VALIDATION RUN, CURRENT STATUS, KNOWN ISSUES, NEXT ACTION)
5. Transfer only when the state is understandable

Do not leave mixed ownership. Serialize overlapping work (auth, schema, lockfiles, shared config). Parallelize only separable surfaces.

